# Docs from source

API reference is generated from docstrings and doc comments, never written by
hand. `docs/build.sh` compiles every language into one site and fails on any
undocumented public symbol or docs warning.

A doc comment is written for someone calling the code who hasn't read its
body:

- **Summary line:** what it does, in the imperative mood.
- **Parameters:** meaning, units, valid range, and what happens outside it.
  Write `rate_hz: sample rate in Hz, > 0`, not `rate: the rate`.
- **Return value and errors:** what is returned, what can be raised or
  returned as an error, and when.
- **Invariants and side effects:** state it mutates, I/O, thread safety, and
  cost when it isn't obvious (for example, O(n²) in the history length).
- **Example:** a short one that runs. Python doctests run under
  `pytest --doctest-modules`, and Rust doc tests under `cargo test --doc`. In
  languages without runnable examples, keep the example minimal and cover the
  same behaviour in a test.

Don't restate the signature or the types, and don't narrate the
implementation. Private helpers need a comment only when a constraint is
non-obvious.

A PR that changes public code, doc comments, or the docs build runs
`mise run docs`, which runs `docs/build.sh` with the pinned tools. It must
exit 0, and the PR lists it under verification. Open
`docs/_build/html/index.html`, or screenshot the pages the PR changed,
including the architecture page in light and dark mode, and attach the
screenshots to the PR. Never commit `docs/_build/` or `docs/_generated/`.

The first code in a repo, and the first file in each new language, add that
language to the build in the same PR. Read the `source-docs` skill before
doing that, and before changing `docs/build.sh`, `docs/conf.py`, or a
documentation gate. For a new language, also read the `add-language` skill,
which covers its checks, CI job, and test command as well as its docs.
